 feelings of  grief and sadness  for some those emotions can manifest  into physical ailments  loneliness actually has wide-ranging  consequences  for the entire body it affects the  immune system  it affects wound healing toronto  emergency room dr  jacques lee has seen this first hand  especially over the last 17 months  loneliness is as bad for you as smoking  cigarettes  in fact lee estimates loneliness kills  45 000 canadians each year  a staggering number he plans to target  with a new trial  beginning in september mount sinai  emergency room patients 70 years plus  who are diagnosed with loneliness  will be able to join a phone call  follow-up program  they work with the individual to  identify  goals that are meaningful to the  individual you know what would improve  their quality of life  volunteers will then coach patients to  reach those goals  a similar program has already found  success in australia  danilo bisdock has spent years studying  loneliness and says while the pandemic  has certainly  exacerbated isolation it's also offered  researchers a unique opportunity  to learn something new about  this capacity that makes humans human a  complex social interaction  back in calgary batala isn't convinced  there's a solution  there will always be loneliness the  human condition she says  and an unfortunate reality of aging  jamie morocco global news toronto  still ahead the odyssey continues for  the elephants  with a travel bug  a mammoth trek taking place in china has  intrigued wildlife  experts in march of 2020 a herd of  elephants left their nature reserve and  they've been on the go since  their slow sojourn has sparked  roadblocks towns and villages had to be  evacuated  and now these pachyderms are heading  home and that has fascinated scientists  even more here's reverend shannon  they are simultaneously lumbering and  majestic the 500 kilometer cross-country  tour of this herd of elephants  has captivated china their naps  their dips their highway strolls  all caught on camera authorities are  tracking  every plodding footstep  even evacuating neighborhoods in their  path the elephant's journey began in  spring of 2020 when they headed north  out of their nature reserve through  villages towns and eventually to the  outskirts of the huge provincial capital  kunming  in recent days they have started to head  south again  wow they just boomed like big news  becky shu chen is an elephant researcher  from kunming  she says these long treks are not  uncommon  but wild elephants in cities are we had  no experience with this ever in the past  the reason for their journey is still  unknown chen  says something may have frightened them  or they could have gained a taste  for the convenience of human grown food  the crops which are more tasty has more  nutrition and also they don't need to  spend like 18 hours  in the forest to looking for the blend  flavored food they can just stay in the  rice view there were made food  you should confuse asian elephant  numbers in china have  finally started climbing again in recent  years  now at about 300. we still want to have  more elephants  but how to help them to coexist with  local communities  especially to the new regions where  elephants  are going back the extensive drone  surveillance is helping researchers  learn more about the animal's behavior  i'm astounded that these these elephants  in china  are so calm around the drones and you  know they're asleep  in lying down they don't get up they  don't  have any reaction elephant expert  phyllis lee is  impressed with the efforts to keep the  animals and humans safe  but her great concern is that the gentle  but sometimes  deadly giants will struggle to find  somewhere to settle  never mind find their way home again  redmond shannon global news  tic london ufo up next the mysterious  object at the center of a u.s military  report  for years ufo enthusiasts have waited  for the day the u.s military opened its  files on possible alien sightings  while a much anticipated report on these  close encounters was finally presented  to america's congress  but as mike delay reports the chances of  finding any solid answers in its pages  remain a mystery  in 2004 the u.s navy saw something on  radar it couldn't explain  one of the planes sent to investigate  was flown by lieutenant commander alex  dietrich  who saw something unexplainable we were  able to  then visually pick up what we  described as a tic tac because that's  what it looked like this  white oblong shaped  object that was moving very fast  dietrich says she's not a ufo person but  she also can't unsee what she saw  and at least now the us intelligence  community is backing up her story  sort of a special report has been  delivered to congress  which details incidents involving the  u.s military encountering  uaps or unidentified aerial phenomena  which ufo researcher victor vigiani says  is their way of avoiding what he  considers  obvious these things are off-world  craft without a doubt the report doesn't  go  that far its major concern is  determining if there is a threat to u.s  national security  it concludes there's no one explanation  for all the incidents  although it does allow that advanced  technologies may have been used  beyond that or just beyond it doesn't  say  i have asked some of our senior  scientists and at least one of them has  stated that he holds the  extraterrestrial hypothesis  just above some of the others so if i  had to make a bet i would bet that it's  something off world but again  i don't have enough data oh my gosh that  this conversation is happening in such a  prominent way was once thought  impossible within the ufo community  considering the number of hoaxes  explainable phenomena credibility has  always proven to be  elusive it's a big step forward there's  no doubt about it's a big step forward  but it's not the complete answer  it's a truth but not the final truth  about what's out there  micro lake global news toronto  and that is global national for this  saturday night i'm robin gill  your canada tonight is a pair of ruddy  ducks in saskatoon  we'd love to see your corner of the  country so please keep sending your  photos to viewers at globalnational.com  thank you for watching and i'll see you  back here tomorrow have a great night  [Music]  you
 

